/* __acad3dV64 (shell unification, step 1): the Canvas board is a MODE, not a backdrop.

   Before this rule, #viewport (the Canvas board: 1600x754 at x=0 in a 1600x950 window) stayed
   display:block in EVERY workspace. Entering Drafting or 3D did not switch to the BIM shell --
   it painted #acad3d (z-index 9500) ON TOP of a still-mounted, still-live Canvas board. The
   three workspaces stacked rather than switched, which is the structural reason the app reads as
   three programs sharing a window rather than one program with three views:

     - two full-window surfaces were mounted at once, disagreeing about the work area (the board
       ran 0..1600 wide and stopped 196px above the window bottom; the BIM shell ran 54..1600 and
       went to the bottom edge -- a 54px origin offset and a 14px height difference between two
       things the user experiences as "the drawing area");
     - the covered board kept its own listeners, its own RAF paint loop and its own hit-testing
       alive underneath an opaque cover, for no benefit;
     - and every "these two panels are fighting" symptom fixed so far (collapsing the file dock on
       entry, hiding the rail's Props/Layers/Blocks buttons -- the rule directly above this one)
       was a patch on that single root cause rather than a fix of it.

   body.a3d-mode is already toggled by enter3d/exit3d, so Canvas restores itself on exit with no
   extra bookkeeping and no second code path to keep in sync. */
body.a3d-mode #viewport{display:none!important}
/* __acad3dV73: the dock's file header ("Canvas Mockup / Drafts / Free") names the
   whiteboard document. With Canvas retired as a workspace there is no such document to
   open, and the drawing's real identity is in the doc tabs above and the inspector's
   Identity Data. The collapse chevron beside it is kept -- it collapses the dock, which
   is still a real thing to do -- so only the title block itself is hidden. */
/* __acad3dV80: the card block was hidden here, leaving the header an empty bar with a chevron
   on it. V80 makes it name the PROJECT and the site, which is what the reference tools put in
   that space, so it is shown again -- at the compact height this rule introduced, not the
   original 70px whiteboard card. The header was never a user-visible leftover; a probe that
   read textContent found the whiteboard's words in an element nobody could see. */
body.a3d-mode #figma-layers-panel .fl-file-head > div:first-child{display:block;min-width:0}
body.a3d-mode #figma-layers-panel .fl-file-title{font-size:12px;font-weight:700;max-width:100%}
body.a3d-mode #figma-layers-panel .fl-file-sub{font-size:10px;margin-top:1px;gap:6px}
