/* __acad3dV65: the Project Browser, once nested, is a SECTION of the dock panel rather than a
   panel of its own -- so it drops the things that made it look like a separate surface (its own
   fixed width, its own right-hand border, its own background) and inherits the column instead.
   The panel becomes the single scroll container, so the rail's content and the BIM navigator
   scroll together as one list, which is the whole point of nesting them. */
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel{overflow-y:auto;overflow-x:hidden}
/* The panel's own content normally uses flex:1 to fill the column, and its inner lists scroll
   independently. That is right when it is the ONLY thing in the panel and wrong the moment it has
   a sibling: a flex:1 child claims the leftover height, the appended tree then lays out on top of
   it, and the two render over each other (observed: the tree's "Properties" heading printed
   through the file tab's "Pages" heading). In the docked state every section sizes to its own
   content instead and the panel is the single scroller -- which is also what makes the sections
   scroll together as one list rather than as several independent viewports. */
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel > .fl-tab-content{flex:0 0 auto;min-height:0}
/* The rail selects WHICH sections the column shows; it does not pile everything into one list.
   Stacking is for sections that belong to the same subject -- which is why the first attempt was
   wrong: it put the Canvas board's own Pages and Layers (its cards: "Quick actions", "Start here")
   directly above the BIM Properties of a selected Room. Those are two different models, so
   stacking them side by side in one scroll is not "united", it is two unrelated lists sharing a
   scrollbar. File now means the BIM navigator while this shell is up, and Assets still means the
   asset library; each is one subject, with its own sections stacked beneath it. */
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-shell[data-tab="file"] #figma-layers-panel > .fl-tab-content{display:none}
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-shell[data-tab="assets"] #figma-layers-panel > .a3d-tree{display:none}
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel .fl-layers,
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel .fl-library,
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel .fl-layer-list{overflow:visible;flex:0 0 auto;min-height:0}
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel > .a3d-tree{
  width:100%!important;flex:1 1 auto;min-height:0;border-right:0;background:transparent;
  border-top:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.08);margin-top:2px;
}
/* __acad3dV66: the tree keeps its own two scroll regions rather than dissolving into the column's
   single scroll. Letting the column scroll once (the V65 behaviour) is what made a selected wall
   push the Project Browser 1132px down: Properties is content-sized and unbounded, so the section
   BELOW it leaves the viewport entirely. The undocked palette already solved this -- propsbody is
   capped at 46% with its own scrollbar and the browser takes the rest -- so docking now inherits
   that instead of overriding it, and both sections stay on screen at every selection size. */
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-shell[data-tab="file"] #figma-layers-panel{overflow:hidden}
/* __acad3dV66's 46% cap on the docked Properties pane is gone with V68: Properties is no
   longer in this column, so there is nothing left here for it to crowd. The Project Browser
   rule below stays -- it is what gives the navigator the full height it now has. */
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel > .a3d-tree > #a3d-browser{
  flex:1 1 auto;min-height:120px;overflow:auto;}
/* Section headers get the quiet, inset treatment the rest of the dock uses, so "Properties" and
   "Project Browser" read as peers of "Cards & notes" rather than as a foreign panel's title bar. */
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel > .a3d-tree .a3d-palhd{
  background:transparent;padding:11px 12px 7px;font-size:12px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:.01em;
}
/* __acad3dV66: the Project Browser header carries seven action buttons. At the palette's old
   268px that row fit on the title line; in the 296px dock, with the title at 12px/900, the last
   two (+Fam, Imp) were clipped off the right edge -- controls that exist but cannot be clicked.
   The header wraps instead, so the buttons drop to their own full-width row and all seven stay
   reachable at any dock width. */
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel > .a3d-tree .a3d-palhd{flex-wrap:wrap;row-gap:5px}
body.a3d-tree-docked #figma-layers-panel > .a3d-tree .a3d-palhd .a3d-palbtns{
  flex-wrap:wrap;flex:1 1 100%;justify-content:flex-start;}
